## Follow-up: ontology-2's possible findings (sub-directory `susp/`)

Each candidate from `$RUN_DIR/ontology-2/proposals/*.json` (`possible_findings`) named by the orchestrator was decided
on AUDITD with scratch data through the product's own entry points (`susp/susp.py`; a stand-in replaced only the
`mcp` transport, which is not installed, and the networkx loader's container connection factory), or by inspection
where asked. Findings in `susp/findings-proposed.json`, merged into `findings/audit/zz-structural.json`.

| Candidate | Decision | Id, severity | Run / evidence |
|---|---|---|---|
| networkx-loader --clear-existing deletes every graph's rows by raw SQL | reproduced-live: every graph's edges, labels, properties gone, nodes, temporal edges and vectors left, strict mode on, no revision | F-C0340, S3 | `st-live-susp-loader-clear` |
| ^NKG class methods called directly answer all graphs merged | reproduced-live: ClosenessGlobal answers both graphs' nodes as one graph, BetweennessGlobal the named graph's; kg_Closeness refuses | F-C1041, S3 | `st-live-susp-nkg-merged` |
| stdio MCP LoadGraph reports 0 nodes and 0 edges | reproduced-live: nodesLoaded 0, edgesLoaded 0 after writing 3 nodes, 2 edges | F-C1640, S4 | `st-live-susp-mcp-load` |
| generated GraphQL `properties` always [] | reproduced-live: [] beside typed fields name, colour | F-C1641, S3 | `st-live-susp-gql-props` |
| IVG.CypherEngine.Scalar answers its default on a failed read | confirmed-source; live input (a failing count answers error, rows []); the call itself blocked: no pydantic in the container's embedded Python | F-C0940, S3 | `st-live-susp-scalar` (blocked) |
| range() with a run-time step of 0 answers [] | reproduced-live: [] with no error; the literal 0 raises NumberOutOfRange | F-C0841, S4 | `st-live-susp-range-step` |
| get_json_field_values pastes the field into SQL | reproduced-live: a quote breaks the statement; a crafted field reads another key's row (read-only) | F-C0341, S2 | `st-live-susp-json-field` |
| enterprise web gateway recipe: management from any address, _SYSTEM/SYS | confirmed-source (inspection only, not deployed) | F-C1741, S3 | `docker/enterprise/webgateway-init.sh:12-31`, `docker-compose-2026.yml:5-6, 28-29` |

## Second follow-up: DDL/dictionary frame oracle and declared-reads extension (`frame2/`, `reads2/`)

Cells re-decided here are in `MATRIX-INPUT-2.json` (they supersede the same (gate, module) cells of the table above).

- 3.6-frame: `frame2/dictoracle.py` snapshots the SQL catalog (INFORMATION_SCHEMA tables, columns, indexes,
  constraints, routines), the class dictionary (%Dictionary.CompiledClass, CompiledStorage) and the global directory
  before and after each action, reads the journal's catalog globals and captures the DDL text the product executes;
  nothing compiled (only scratch `IVGAudit.*` tables for bystanders and seeds). Self-test `st-control-frame2-selftest`
  (seeded scratch table, scratch global and second triple caught). 11 DDL-issuing or so-far-unobserved actions
  (`st-live-frame2-run`): setup re-run, the FETCH FIRST probe, route creation, BUILD INDEX, Cypher schema commands,
  dbapi_utils index helpers and upserts, post-commit overwrite, temporal-row retracts, retract_inference, adoption
  UPDATE: all hold. Closed now: 01, 02, 03, 04, 05, 06, 08, 10 (fails, F-C1001), 11, 12, 13, 14, 17. Still open: 07
  (3.2.0 rescue statements; kg_TXT procedures not re-created by a setup re-run), 09 (native executor plan compilation,
  out of scope), 15 (FHIR DDL, no FHIR classes).
- 3.4-declared-reads: the non-interference oracle (`reads2/ni2.py`, self-test `st-control-reads2-selftest`) over the
  entry points left unchecked: 03 (18 actions), 11 (15 actions), 12, 13, 14, 18 (13 actions) all hold
  (`st-live-reads2-03-11`, `st-live-reads2-12-18`); 20 pure helpers justified statically (`reads2/static.json`).
  Closed now: 03 (fails, F-C0341), 11, 12, 13, 14, 18. 10 stays open but fails on F-C1041; 04 and 10 entry points
  (113 and 200) were not extended.
- No new findings: setup's __graph move is F-C0150 (writesets lane), execute_sql(read_only=True) writing is HD-1701.

## Not findings, reported for decision

- Lock escalation across graphs (capkeys): one caller transaction with more than about 1,000 row writes per table
  makes every other process's write to that table, in any graph, fail after 10 s with SQLCODE -110 (`st-live-cap-batch`).
  Answers stay consistent; whether one tenant may block another is not stated anywhere (intent-undecided).
- A count aggregate of 4 for 3 temporal events after `keys.py`'s combined sequence (`st-live-keys`), not repeated in 18
  isolated trials (`st-live-keys-temporal-count`, `st-live-keys-temporal-race`).
- Reused, unregistered first-attempt logs are listed in `capkeys/method.json` (`unregistered_logs`) and the order
  sub-lane's superseded results in `order/method.json` (`superseded`).
